Keep _lseek_r at the width its caller was built with

With picolibc, esp_libc's lseek() is the function the prebuilt library's fdopen()
and fseek() bind to by name, and it calls _lseek_r. That library was compiled with
a 32-bit off_t, so lseek() -- and therefore its call into _lseek_r -- passes 32
bits. _lseek_r here is an alias for esp_vfs_lseek, whose signature follows off_t:
right while off_t is 32 bits, and wrong as soon as a project widens it, when the
offset's high half and the whence are read from registers nobody set and every
fseek() lands somewhere else.

That is not hypothetical. With off_t widened to 64 bits, a 12 KB SFTP upload fails
with "seek failed", because the write path seeks to the end of the file before
each chunk and lands past it.

So _lseek_r keeps the width its caller has -- a 32-bit boundary, widening inside
it -- and stops being an alias for esp_vfs_lseek. A result that does not fit fails
with EOVERFLOW, as a 32-bit lseek() does on Linux. A project that widens off_t
reaches the 64-bit esp_vfs_lseek through its own lseek64, which is where a file
over 4 GiB is actually reached. stdio's own seeking stays limited to 4 GiB; that
is a property of the prebuilt libc, not of this change.

The better fix on this side is a Kconfig knob for 64-bit off_t -- glibc's
_FILE_OFFSET_BITS -- which would make the width explicit instead of implied, and
let IDF provide the lseek64 half itself. There is none today.

diff --git a/components/vfs/vfs_calls.c b/components/vfs/vfs_calls.c
index a8c46954..d678aa94 100644
--- a/components/vfs/vfs_calls.c
+++ b/components/vfs/vfs_calls.c
@@ -934,8 +934,27 @@ ssize_t pread(int fd, void *dst, size_t size, off_t offset)
     __attribute__((alias("esp_vfs_pread")));
 ssize_t pwrite(int fd, const void *src, size_t size, off_t offset)
     __attribute__((alias("esp_vfs_pwrite")));
-off_t _lseek_r(struct _reent *r, int fd, off_t size, int mode)
-    __attribute__((alias("esp_vfs_lseek")));
+/*
+ * A 32-bit ABI, deliberately, and not an alias for esp_vfs_lseek.
+ *
+ * Its caller is esp_libc's lseek(), the one the prebuilt picolibc's fdopen() and
+ * fseek() bind to by name, and that library was compiled with a 32-bit off_t. A
+ * project may widen off_t; if this followed the type, a 32-bit offset would be
+ * read as 64 and every fseek() would land somewhere else. So the boundary keeps
+ * its width and the widening happens here.
+ */
+int _lseek_r(struct _reent *r, int fd, int offset, int whence)
+{
+    const off_t at = esp_vfs_lseek(r, fd, (off_t)offset, whence);
+
+    /* Round-trip rather than a limit constant: it is the same question, and it
+     * needs no header. */
+    if (at != (off_t)(int)at) {
+        errno = EOVERFLOW;
+        return -1;
+    }
+    return (int)at;
+}
 int _fcntl_r(struct _reent *r, int fd, int cmd, int arg)
     __attribute__((alias("esp_vfs_fcntl_r")));
 int _fstat_r(struct _reent *r, int fd, struct stat *st)
